Elastic Load Balancer:

Application Load Balancer(ALB): ALB is a great fit for microservices & container-based applications.
- Routing to a different tg.
- Routing based on path in URL (example: example.com/user & example.com/feed)
- Routing based on hostname in URL (ex: one.example.com & others.example.com)
- Routing based on query string, headers (ex: example.com/user?id=123 & order=false)


Network Load Balancer(NLB): NLB has one static IP per AZ and supports assigning an Elastic IP (helpful for whitelisting the specific IP).
-NLB works on layer 4 allow to:
Forward TCP & UDP traffic to your instances
-Handles millions of requests per second.
- Ultra-low latency.
 
Gateway Load Balancer(GLB): Deploy, scale, and manage a fleet of 3rd party network virtual applications in AWS. e.g., firewall, intrusion detection and prevention systems, Deep packet inspection systems, payload manipulation.
- Operates at layer 3(Network layer) - IP packets
- Combine the following functions:
- Transparent Network Gateway - single entry/exit for all traffic
- Load balancer - distributes traffic to your virtual appliances.
- Uses the GENEVE protocol on port 6081
Exam: Suppose if we want to impose some security practices for users requesting to access the application, but we want to validate the traffic via our 3rd party security tool and pass that traffic to the application after validation. 
